7dro|v|To shift or turn from one side to the other, from the direct course, or from truth; to speak with equivocation; to shuffle; to quibble; as, he prevaricates in his statement. ; To collude, as where an informer colludes with the defendant, and makes a sham prosecution. ; To undertake a thing falsely and deceitfully, with the purpose of defeating or destroying it. ; To evade by a quibble; to transgress; to pervert.	D:aix1,18,5~aix1,18,5
7drq|n|of Prevaricate	v:93lv,72,4~93lv,72,4
7drr|n|The act of prevaricating, shuffling, or quibbling, to evade the truth or the disclosure of truth; a deviation from the truth and fair dealing. ; A secret abuse in the exercise of a public office. ; The collusion of an informer with the defendant, for the purpose of making a sham prosecution. ; A false or deceitful seeming to undertake a thing for the purpose of defeating or destroying it.	n:alxf,54,5~alxf,54,5
